--- A function handed a value that failed, for a parameter that cannot
--- fail, hands the error on as its own answer (spec §10,
--- auto-propagation), whatever the function is named: `h3`, `p`, `a` are
--- the element vocabulary inside a view and the program's own functions
--- elsewhere. The call is typed as able to fail, so `.ok` of its answer
--- asks the answer, an operator on the answer carries the error, and
--- inside a function the error goes back to the caller. (Both modes had
--- typed a call of a function named like an element as its plain
--- return: the interpreter decided `.ok` true of an answer that was the
--- error, and the compiled program did not build. The fuzzer's helpers
--- are named h1, h2, which is how it was found.)
fn h3[x]
    return x + 1

fn p[s]
    k = h3[float[s]]
    return k * 2

fn plus[x]
    return x + 1

t = float['x']
w = h3[t]
log[w]
log[w.ok]
log[w.kind]
log[w + 1]
r = p['z']
log[r]
log[r.ok]
log[p['2']]
u = plus[h3[float['y']]]
log[u]
log[u.ok]
good = h3[2.5]
log[good]
log[good.ok]
if not w.ok
    log['w failed: `w.reason`']
